<section id="conclusions">
  <div class="section-label">Section 6</div>
  <h2>Conclusions &amp; Limitations</h2>

  <h3>Key Findings Summary</h3>
  <div class="two-col">
    <p><strong>Finding 1 — Structural Stability with a Mid-Period Shift.</strong> The BIEK Computer Science exam has maintained a highly stable structure: exactly 15 MCQs and 3 Long Questions per year across all 10 available years. The only meaningful structural variation is in Short Questions, which increased from 9 to 18 between 2016 and 2017, then contracted to 15 from 2021 onward.</p>
    <p><strong>Finding 2 — Three Structurally Distinct Periods.</strong> Rather than one stable exam, the data reveals three distinct periods: Period A (2015–2016, 27 questions), Period B (2017–2019, 36 questions), and Period C (2021–2025, 33 questions). This suggests deliberate policy changes in the exam blueprint at least twice.</p>
    <p><strong>Finding 3 — Topic Concentration is Extreme.</strong> Three topic clusters (Control Structures, Database Concepts, C Programming Fundamentals + Data Types) account for the overwhelming majority of question content. File Handling, by contrast, is persistently under-represented. This concentration gives educators a clear signal about where to focus curriculum time.</p>
    <p><strong>Finding 4 — Long Questions are Perfectly Stable.</strong> With a standard deviation of 0 across all 10 years, the Long Question count is the most statistically invariant feature of the entire dataset. This suggests the Long Question paper is tightly controlled by the board's paper-setting committee and reflects a deliberate, fixed allocation.</p>
    <p><strong>Finding 5 — Database Models are Rising.</strong> While Database Models as a category shows a lower overall mention count (51), it has actually increased in recent years (2022: 9, 2023: 7, 2024: 7) compared to early years (2015: 2, 2019: 3). This may reflect a curriculum emphasis shift toward theoretical database knowledge in later years.</p>
    <p><strong>Finding 6 — 2020 is a Data Gap.</strong> No data exists for the 2020 examination year. This is almost certainly due to COVID-19 examination cancellations. Any period-level trend analysis should acknowledge this gap explicitly.</p>
  </div>

